    "response": " To evaluate the integral \\(\\int_0^1 \\frac{5x^4}{1 + x^5} \\, dx\\), we can use the method of substitution. Let's set \\(u = 1 + x^5\\). Then, the differential \\(du\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ du = 5x^4 \\, dx \\]\n\nThis substitution transforms the integral into a simpler form. Notice that when \\(x = 0\\), \\(u = 1 + 0^5 = 1\\), and when \\(x = 1\\), \\(u = 1 + 1^5 = 2\\). Therefore, the integral becomes:\n\n\\[ \\int_0^1 \\frac{5x^4}{1 + x^5} \\, dx = \\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{u} \\, du \\]\n\nThe integral of \\(\\frac{1}{u}\\) is \\(\\ln|u|\\). So, we have:\n\n\\[ \\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{u} \\, du = \\left[ \\ln|u| \\right]_1^2 = \\ln 2 - \\ln 1 \\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln 1 = 0\\), the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[ \\ln 2 - 0 = \\ln 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the integral is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\ln 2} \\]",
